WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.972
Hello and welcome. I'm James Murphy. 

00:00:01.972 --> 00:00:06.585
And welcome to the mind-bending world of Python metaclasses.

00:00:06.585 --> 00:00:11.075
Metaclasses let you break barriers and assumptions that you had about Python.

00:00:11.075 --> 00:00:13.687
Consider the example of function overloads.

00:00:13.687 --> 00:00:18.812
If you've spent a little bit of time with Python, then you know that Python doesn't have function overloads.

00:00:18.812 --> 00:00:22.475
Here, I try to define this function f three different times.

00:00:22.475 --> 00:00:27.642
But actually the second definition just overwrites the first and the third overwrites the second.

00:00:27.642 --> 00:00:32.439
So, at the end here, there's just one copy of f, the last one.

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:35.639
You could see this by trying to call the function with just one argument.

00:00:35.639 --> 00:00:38.542
If you do, you end up with an error.

00:00:38.542 --> 00:00:40.733
The typing module has a decorator named overload.

00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:42.797
But that doesn't actually solve the problem. 

00:00:42.797 --> 00:00:45.103
So, I would still get an error if I were to run this.

00:00:45.103 --> 00:00:50.020
But what if I told you that with a metaclass you can actually get true overloading.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:53.993
Just to show you how awesome metaclasses are, that's exactly what I did.

00:00:53.993 --> 00:00:58.448
Now, when I run the example, if I call f with an int, it calls the int overload.

00:00:58.448 --> 00:01:01.086
If I call f with a string, it calls the string overload.

00:01:01.086 --> 00:01:04.327
And if I call it with multiple arguments, it calls the two argument overload.

00:01:04.327 --> 00:01:05.322
I know what you're thinking.

00:01:05.322 --> 00:01:10.905
This is nothing short of mind-bending, face-melting wizardry and you're not totally wrong.

00:01:10.905 --> 00:01:13.202
Before we can understand this example,

00:01:13.202 --> 00:01:16.916
let's go back and understand just what is a metaclass in the first place.

00:01:16.916 --> 00:01:20.448
Oh and by the way, I'm giving out some professional licenses to PyCharm

00:01:20.448 --> 00:01:23.355
that's this editor that I always use in my videos.

00:01:23.355 --> 00:01:28.837
Be sure to stick around at the end to see how you can get a chance at winning a professional license for free.

00:01:28.837 --> 00:01:31.222
Every object in Python has a type.

00:01:31.222 --> 00:01:39.089
42 is an int, hello is a string, the empty list is a list and an instance of my class A is an A.

00:01:39.089 --> 00:01:42.976
Classes allow you to make instances of that class.

00:01:42.976 --> 00:01:45.881
But classes are also objects themselves.

00:01:45.881 --> 00:01:48.346
So, what's the type of a class?

00:01:48.346 --> 00:01:53.385
In all of these examples, the type of the class is a type

00:01:53.385 --> 00:01:54.820
which kind of makes sense.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:02:00.632
And just like 42 is an instance of an int, int is an instance of a type.

00:02:00.632 --> 00:02:04.355
At runtime, I can use int to create new integer objects.

00:02:04.355 --> 00:02:09.812
And just the same, at runtime, I can use type to create new type objects.

00:02:09.812 --> 00:02:13.909
By the way, if you're wondering about what's the difference between a type and a class,

00:02:13.909 --> 00:02:15.781
in Python 3 there's no difference.

00:02:15.781 --> 00:02:20.095
So, If I say something is a type or something is a class, I mean exactly the same thing.

00:02:20.095 --> 00:02:23.890
As concepts of the language, type and class are identical.

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:27.290
But literally in the language, they mean different things.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:31.429
class is a keyword which you use for constructing types.

00:02:31.429 --> 00:02:35.903
It turns out that a class definition is actually just syntactic sugar

00:02:35.903 --> 00:02:39.476
for a specific way of calling the type constructor.

00:02:39.476 --> 00:02:42.302
Instead of using a normal class definition,

00:02:42.302 --> 00:02:46.969
I could equally well call the type constructor directly doing something like this.

00:02:46.969 --> 00:02:51.812
I just need the name of the type, a tuple of bases that it's inheriting from

00:02:51.812 --> 00:02:53.906
and then I run the class body

00:02:53.906 --> 00:02:59.268
and then store those definitions from the class body in a dictionary called the namespace

00:02:59.268 --> 00:03:04.136
then I feed those things into the type constructor and out pops a new class A.

00:03:04.136 --> 00:03:07.391
A slightly more accurate picture would look something like this though.

00:03:07.391 --> 00:03:10.094
Again, I need the name and bases of the class,

00:03:10.094 --> 00:03:16.256
then the namespace or class dictionary is created using this double under prepare method.

00:03:16.256 --> 00:03:22.490
The body of the class just gets treated as code which gets executed directly in this new dictionary namespace.

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:27.735
Metaclasses are a point of customization in this class creation process.

00:03:27.735 --> 00:03:31.928
A metaclass is just a class that inherits from the type class.

00:03:31.928 --> 00:03:37.515
That means, just like type, the job of a metaclass is to create classes.

00:03:37.515 --> 00:03:44.234
By defining our own metaclass, we'll be able to customize things like how the class namespace is created using prepare

00:03:44.234 --> 00:03:48.093
or things that happen in the init or new methods of the metaclass.

00:03:48.093 --> 00:03:51.605
The default metaclass, if you don't specify, is type.

00:03:51.605 --> 00:03:54.332
So, by changing the metaclass of a class,

00:03:54.332 --> 00:03:58.920
that means we're just going to substitute our metaclass in everywhere that you see type here.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.052
Here's the simplest possible example of metaclass.

00:04:02.052 --> 00:04:04.221
Here, I just have a blank class.

00:04:04.221 --> 00:04:07.698
As we've already seen, if I create an instance of the class,

00:04:07.698 --> 00:04:12.047
the type of a is capital A, the class.

00:04:12.047 --> 00:04:15.826
But the type of the class is type.

00:04:15.826 --> 00:04:17.977
Now, let's change the metaclass.

00:04:17.977 --> 00:04:21.246
A metaclass is just a class that inherits from type.

00:04:21.246 --> 00:04:27.355
You can change the metaclass of a class just by specifying it as a keyword argument in the class definition.

00:04:27.355 --> 00:04:32.689
So, the type of capital A is now my metaclass, instead of type.

00:04:32.689 --> 00:04:36.214
This gives another way to think about what a metaclass is.

00:04:36.214 --> 00:04:40.057
A metaclass is just the type of a type or the class of a class.

00:04:40.057 --> 00:04:43.183
If you want to know at runtime what's the metaclass of a class,

00:04:43.183 --> 00:04:45.473
then just call type on the actual class.

00:04:45.473 --> 00:04:50.295
Calling type on an instance little a, tells you what class created the little a.

00:04:50.295 --> 00:04:52.897
So, little a was created by big A.

00:04:52.897 --> 00:04:56.711
Similarly, the big A itself was created by the metaclass.

00:04:56.711 --> 00:04:59.883
So, type of big A is the metaclass.

00:04:59.883 --> 00:05:01.242
Here's a simple example.

00:05:01.242 --> 00:05:05.976
LoadTimeMeta automatically adds a new variable onto the class when it's created.

00:05:05.976 --> 00:05:10.048
It just computes how many seconds after some initial base time the class was created

00:05:10.048 --> 00:05:12.718
and then stores that as a class variable.

00:05:12.718 --> 00:05:16.312
Whatever we do in new, we do eventually need to make a super call

00:05:16.312 --> 00:05:20.499
and pass all of the arguments up to type so that it can actually create the class.

00:05:20.499 --> 00:05:25.210
And here, we can see that we actually do have the class_load_time automatically added into the class.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:28.141
If I didn't have LoadTimeMeta as the metaclass,

00:05:28.141 --> 00:05:31.132
then this would just crash because that wouldn't be defined.

00:05:31.132 --> 00:05:36.140
Another extremely important property of metaclasses is that, they are inherited.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:41.624
B inherits the metaclass of A and therefore it automatically has its own class_load_time.

00:05:41.624 --> 00:05:44.105
Notice that, the class_load_time is different than the one in A.

00:05:44.105 --> 00:05:46.801
So, it's not just looking up the value that was in A.

00:05:46.801 --> 00:05:50.711
This is an important distinction between how metaclasses are used

00:05:50.711 --> 00:05:53.256
and how something like a class decorator would be used.

00:05:53.256 --> 00:05:56.569
You could use a class decorator to add something simple like this:

00:05:56.569 --> 00:05:58.673
just a class variable onto the class.

00:05:58.673 --> 00:06:01.242
But you wouldn't be able to make that property inheritable.

00:06:01.242 --> 00:06:05.385
If you're not doing something that's supposed to apply to a whole hierarchy of classes,

00:06:05.385 --> 00:06:08.859
then there's a good chance that it could just be done using class decorators.

00:06:08.859 --> 00:06:13.049
So, what's an example where you would want to modify an entire class hierarchy?

00:06:13.049 --> 00:06:17.182
Well, how about a familiar example: abstract base classes.

00:06:17.182 --> 00:06:22.183
Here's a stripped-down version of how abstract base classes in Python actually work.

00:06:22.183 --> 00:06:26.983
Basically, you just inherit from ABC and then you mark some functions with abstract method.

00:06:26.983 --> 00:06:30.125
As long as there is at least one function that's still marked abstract,

00:06:30.125 --> 00:06:32.568
you're not allowed to create an instance of the class.

00:06:32.568 --> 00:06:36.328
So, I wouldn't be allowed to create an a because f and g are abstract.

00:06:36.328 --> 00:06:42.158
But then I define class B that inherits from A and defines concrete implementations for f and g.

00:06:42.158 --> 00:06:45.000
So, I would be able to create an instance of B.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.603
First, this abstract method decorator.

00:06:47.603 --> 00:06:54.865
Well, all it really does is set a secret attribute called isabstract equal to true on the function and then return it back.

00:06:54.865 --> 00:06:58.060
Then in the metaclass, we override dunder call.

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:01.900
Dunder call is called whenever you try to create an instance of the class.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:06.029
The default implementation of dunder call is just going to call new to create the object

00:07:06.029 --> 00:07:08.687
and then initialize it using the init method.

00:07:08.687 --> 00:07:11.043
In ABCMeta, we just change that.

00:07:11.043 --> 00:07:14.529
First, we get a list of all the methods that are still marked abstract

00:07:14.529 --> 00:07:16.918
and then if there are any that are still marked abstract

00:07:16.918 --> 00:07:19.246
then we just refuse to create an instance.

00:07:19.246 --> 00:07:22.975
Otherwise, we just use super to call the normal instantiation process.

00:07:22.975 --> 00:07:25.235
Another common thing that you'll see is,

00:07:25.235 --> 00:07:29.121
creating basically a blank class that does nothing but inherit from the metaclass.

00:07:29.121 --> 00:07:34.321
Doing this allows your users to inherit from that class to change the metaclass without even knowing it.

00:07:34.321 --> 00:07:36.745
It's mostly just a convenience factor.

00:07:36.745 --> 00:07:38.721
The ABC example was kind of mundane.

00:07:38.721 --> 00:07:41.240
All it did is prevent instantiation of a class.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.369
It didn't really modify anything during class creation.

00:07:44.369 --> 00:07:46.857
And that's how a lot of metaclasses are.

00:07:46.857 --> 00:07:48.563
They do just pretty mundane things.

00:07:48.563 --> 00:07:52.875
Metaclasses alone are not this big scary thing that you have to be confused about.

00:07:52.875 --> 00:07:57.114
So, how did metaclasses get their face-melting, gut-punching reputation?

00:07:57.114 --> 00:07:59.424
There's no limit to what you can do with metaclasses.

00:07:59.424 --> 00:08:03.722
So naturally, they start to harbor all of your most mind-bending ideas.

00:08:03.722 --> 00:08:04.819
So, let's get to it.

00:08:04.819 --> 00:08:08.696
How do I add function overloads into Python using a metaclass?

00:08:08.696 --> 00:08:11.667
The key to all of this is the prepare method.

00:08:11.667 --> 00:08:16.456
During the creation of a class like A, the body of the class is executed.

00:08:16.456 --> 00:08:22.269
And the local variables that are defined in the body are saved into a dictionary.

00:08:22.269 --> 00:08:28.173
If you define a prepare method, then its return value is used as that dictionary. 

00:08:28.173 --> 00:08:32.281
In a normal dictionary, if I set f to be this function and then set it again to be this function

00:08:32.281 --> 00:08:34.223
and then set it again to be this function,

00:08:34.223 --> 00:08:39.056
at the end of it, it would just be this last function. The value of f would just be this last thing.

00:08:39.056 --> 00:08:43.668
But what if I created my own dictionary that when you set a value multiple times,

00:08:43.668 --> 00:08:45.585
it doesn't just overwrite the previous value.

00:08:45.585 --> 00:08:48.586
Instead, it creates a list of all the values that it's seen.

00:08:48.586 --> 00:08:51.395
That's the basic idea of this overload dictionary.

00:08:51.395 --> 00:08:52.751
So, that's what I do.

00:08:52.751 --> 00:08:57.481
First thing the overload decorator just sets the secret value dunder overload to true.

00:08:57.481 --> 00:09:01.982
Then in the overload dictionary, if it sees something with dunder overload equal to true,

00:09:01.982 --> 00:09:04.850
it's going to store those values in a list.

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:06.643
I actually define my own OverloadList

00:09:06.643 --> 00:09:12.831
which is just used to tell the difference between whether someone was just trying to store a normal list as a class variable

00:09:12.831 --> 00:09:15.217
versus if someone's trying to overload functions.

00:09:15.217 --> 00:09:19.488
The first time that I try to set a value for a given key in the overload dictionary,

00:09:19.488 --> 00:09:24.203
I either just put in the value as normal if it's not marked as overloaded

00:09:24.203 --> 00:09:27.055
or I put it in a singleton overload list.

00:09:27.055 --> 00:09:31.410
If I try to set a value for a key that already exists in my overload dictionary,

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:34.401
and that key is associated with an overload list

00:09:34.401 --> 00:09:36.367
then I just append the value to the list.

00:09:36.367 --> 00:09:41.290
So, every time I define an overload function f, it just gets appended onto this list.

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:45.176
If I try to set a value in a key that's not associated with an overload list,

00:09:45.176 --> 00:09:47.438
then I just overwrite it like a normal dictionary does.

00:09:47.438 --> 00:09:49.520
Here's how you could use an overload dictionary.

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:54.158
Here, I define an overload function f and then I just set some values in the dictionary.

00:09:54.158 --> 00:09:56.936
So, I set the key a to be two different values.

00:09:56.936 --> 00:10:02.173
But in the final dictionary, it just has the last value because none of these values were marked as overload.

00:10:02.173 --> 00:10:05.782
Since f is marked as overload, when I add it to the dictionary twice,

00:10:05.782 --> 00:10:10.900
instead of overwriting the previous one, it creates a list and keeps track of both of them.

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:15.215
So, by changing A's metaclass to OverloadMeta and marking the functions as overload,

00:10:15.215 --> 00:10:20.399
now when I execute the class body, all three of those definitions of functions are going to be stored.

00:10:20.399 --> 00:10:25.940
So by using the overload dictionary, I can keep track of all the functions but they're just stored in a list.

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:29.600
So, at runtime, how do I actually call one of the functions?

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:31.023
That's what new accomplishes.

00:10:31.023 --> 00:10:35.000
The namespace that gets passed to new is going to be this overload dictionary

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:39.509
that was created in the prepare method after the body of the class has executed.

00:10:39.509 --> 00:10:45.273
What I can do is just look through the namespace and replace any overload lists with overload descriptors.

00:10:45.273 --> 00:10:50.395
Descriptors are what famously allow you to quote own the dot in Python.

00:10:50.395 --> 00:10:55.675
A descriptor is any class that defines a dunder get, set or delete method.

00:10:55.675 --> 00:11:02.019
If you set some value of a class equal to a descriptor, it changes how gets and sets on that value work.

00:11:02.019 --> 00:11:05.765
If I create an s and then execute s dot some_val equals 5,

00:11:05.765 --> 00:11:11.791
it'll actually call the descriptor's set method with the instance being s and the value being 5.

00:11:11.791 --> 00:11:15.206
Similarly, s dot some_val will call the get method.

00:11:15.206 --> 00:11:19.833
If you've ever wondered about how @property works, this is exactly what it's doing.

00:11:19.833 --> 00:11:23.633
So, a descriptor will let me intercept attribute access on a class.

00:11:23.633 --> 00:11:25.993
So, let's make an overload descriptor.

00:11:25.993 --> 00:11:30.465
When you try to look up an overload descriptor, it'll return to you a callable thing

00:11:30.465 --> 00:11:33.643
that knows how to pick one of the functions in the overload list.

00:11:33.643 --> 00:11:37.375
The overload descriptor takes in the list of possible function overloads.

00:11:37.375 --> 00:11:41.770
It also stores the signatures of all those functions using the inspect module.

00:11:41.770 --> 00:11:44.114
When you try to access the overload descriptor,

00:11:44.114 --> 00:11:49.407
I'm going to return some kind of object that's bound to the instance that knows about all of the overloads.

00:11:49.407 --> 00:11:51.910
I have to split things up into another class

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:55.566
because attribute access the time when you access this function

00:11:55.566 --> 00:11:58.569
doesn't have to be the same time when you call the function.

00:11:58.569 --> 00:12:02.167
This acts exactly the same way that normal function lookup behaves.

00:12:02.167 --> 00:12:08.113
Here, if I have a class A with a function f, I can assign a variable func to a dot f.

00:12:08.113 --> 00:12:13.490
But if little a is an instance of the class and I try to store funk equals a dot f,

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:16.219
then func is going to be this bound method.

00:12:16.219 --> 00:12:20.000
This bound method just remembers the little a so that when you call it,

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:23.637
it calls basically a dot f with whatever arguments you pass in.

00:12:23.637 --> 00:12:25.617
When you construct a bound overload dispatcher,

00:12:25.617 --> 00:12:30.410
it just remembers the instance and a bunch of other things like which overloads and their signatures.

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:34.849
When you go to actually call the dispatcher, it uses some algorithm best_match

00:12:34.849 --> 00:12:39.692
in order to decide which of the functions in the overload list is the best match.

00:12:39.692 --> 00:12:45.309
Then it goes ahead and calls that function with its remembered instance and the same args and keyword args.

00:12:45.309 --> 00:12:48.034
I just did the simplest possible thing for finding the best match.

00:12:48.034 --> 00:12:52.619
Basically, loop over all the possible overloads, try to bind the arguments to the signature

00:12:52.619 --> 00:12:57.732
and the first one that passes and matches the types then I just call that the best match.

00:12:57.732 --> 00:12:58.945
So, let's see it in action.

00:12:58.945 --> 00:13:02.035
A is a class with metaclass overload meta.

00:13:02.035 --> 00:13:04.213
It has three overloads of the function f.

00:13:04.213 --> 00:13:07.658
One takes an int, one takes a string and one takes two arguments.

00:13:07.658 --> 00:13:12.258
Looking at capital A dot f, we see that it's an overload with three functions in it.

00:13:12.258 --> 00:13:16.045
But for little a dot f, it returns a bound overload dispatcher.

00:13:16.045 --> 00:13:19.284
So, this object remembers little a and when you call it,

00:13:19.284 --> 00:13:24.063
it will call one of the three functions with little a and whatever other parameters.

00:13:24.063 --> 00:13:27.619
When I call little a.f(1), it calls the int overload.

00:13:27.619 --> 00:13:30.332
a.f("hello") calls the string overload,

00:13:30.332 --> 00:13:33.698
a.f(1, 2) calls the two argument overload

00:13:33.698 --> 00:13:36.951
and a.f(None) doesn't match any of the signatures

00:13:36.951 --> 00:13:39.467
so it raises a no matching overload exception.

00:13:39.467 --> 00:13:45.000
Well, as you could probably guess, using descriptors and checking signatures and type checking at runtime,

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:46.440
it's a big slowdown.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:50.311
Let's compare an overload class versus just a function

00:13:50.311 --> 00:13:53.645
that checks its arguments and does two different things based off of that.

00:13:53.645 --> 00:13:57.886
In this case, using this overload framework is basically causing a 1.5 times slowdown.

00:13:57.886 --> 00:13:59.555
So, that's not very good.

00:13:59.555 --> 00:14:03.415
If I change the implementation to something quick like just returning 0,

00:14:03.415 --> 00:14:06.041
then it turns out to be 81 times slower.

00:14:06.041 --> 00:14:11.034
So yeah, doing all this signature inspecting and type checking at runtime is extremely expensive.

00:14:11.034 --> 00:14:13.938
Now I know the overload thing was the coolest part of the video

00:14:13.938 --> 00:14:17.339
but I can't let you go without letting you know about another application:

00:14:17.339 --> 00:14:18.653
Code Generation.

00:14:18.653 --> 00:14:21.662
This is not how data classes are actually implemented in Python.

00:14:21.662 --> 00:14:23.529
They're implemented using decorators.

00:14:23.529 --> 00:14:26.008
But here's just an example of another way you could do it.

00:14:26.008 --> 00:14:28.394
Let's define a DataClassMeta(type).

00:14:28.394 --> 00:14:31.710
When you construct a new class, it looks for any annotations on the class.

00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:34.400
Then it makes an init function.

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:39.512
By that, I mean it makes a string which is the string representation of the init function.

00:14:39.512 --> 00:14:44.885
So, I make the literal code text from this make init function based off of the annotations

00:14:44.885 --> 00:14:47.177
and then I just go ahead and execute that code.

00:14:47.177 --> 00:14:51.400
That allows me to do something like this where I just define annotations on a function

00:14:51.400 --> 00:14:53.747
